      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate efficacy and safety of enoxaparin for catheter-related arterial thrombosis in infants with congenital heart disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective observational study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Pediatric Intensive Care and Cardiology Unit at the University Children's Hospital of Zurich."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "A cohort of 32[Symbol: see text]infants aged 0-12[Symbol: see text]months treated with enoxaparin for catheter-related arterial thrombosis from 2002 to 2005."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Dose requirements of enoxaparin, resolution of thrombosis by Doppler ultrasound, and bleeding complications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Catheter-related arterial thrombosis was located in the iliac/femoral arteries in 31 (97%) infants and aorta in 1 infant, and was related to indwelling catheters and cardiac catheterization in 17 (53%) and 15 (47%) cases, respectively. Newborns required increased doses of enoxaparin to achieve therapeutic anti-FXa levels (mean 1.62[Symbol: see text]mg/kg per dose) compared with infants aged 2-12 months (mean 1.12 mg/kg per dose; p=0.0002). Complete resolution of arterial thrombosis occurred in 29 (91%) infants at a mean of 23 days after initiation of enoxaparin therapy. Partial or no resolution was observed in 1 (3%) and 2 (6%) infants, respectively, at a mean follow-up time of 4.3 months. Bleeding complications occurred in 1 (3%) infant."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Enoxaparin is efficient and safe for infants with congenital heart disease and catheter-related arterial thrombosis, possibly representing a valid alternative to the currently recommended unfractionated heparin."
        }
      ]},
